// The lockfile — the best answer in the tree to a question this directory has been asking all day,
// and the one place that answer is given away again in the next twenty lines.
//
// ## What `packages/wacpkg/src/lock.wac` gets right, and it is the hard part
//
// A lockfile's rule is *an ordinary build may create a missing entry and must never advance an
// existing valid one*. That is an obligation on a caller, and every other package in this tree writes
// obligations as prose — `ssh`'s window adjust, `quic`'s frame epochs, `git`'s ordering. This file
// does not:
//
//   > That is the whole point of a lockfile and it is the easy thing to get wrong, because
//   > **"resolve the ref" is the same operation in both cases and only the surrounding decision
//   > differs.** So the decision is made here, by `plan`, and it is **a value a caller cannot ignore
//   > rather than a comment telling it what not to do.**
//
// `plan(Manifest, Lock)` is pure — *"No I/O, and no network … this decides *whether* it may, which is
// the part with the rule in it"* — and hands back one decision per mapping. A caller that wants to
// resolve a ref has to have been given a step that says so.
//
// **That is the constructor obligation `@/packages/quic/src/params.wac` said would be needed and did
// not find.** An entry written an hour before this one asked what a language would have to provide to
// beat a predicate, answered *"something that makes the call unforgettable"*, and called it a
// constructor obligation. It needs no language feature at all: separate the decision from the action,
// return the decision, and the action cannot be reached without it. Recorded as a correction to that
// entry rather than as a new ask.
//
// ## And then the decision is an `i32` with two fields that are usually meaningless
//
//     export i32 USE()     { return 0; }
//     export i32 CREATE()  { return 1; }
//     export i32 REFRESH() { return 2; }
//
//     export struct Step {
//       string name;
//       i32 action;
//       string commit;    // set only for USE
//       string why;       // for REFRESH, which input changed; "" otherwise
//     }
//
// Three actions as bare integers, and **each payload field is valid for exactly one of them**. A
// caller reading `step.commit` after a `CREATE` gets `""`, which is not a commit and is not a
// diagnostic either.
//
// So the file wins the argument it set out to win — the decision is a value — and then hands back the
// part the value was for. The two are independent: **getting the obligation right does not carry you
// through the encoding**, and this is the clearest demonstration of that in the tree, because the
// same author did both, twenty lines apart, and was explicitly thinking about the first.

import { Result, Bytes, Vec } from "core";

/** A resolved commit — forty hex characters, and not a ref. */
export struct Commit { string sha; }

/**
 * What to do about one mapping.
 *
 * The payload travels with the decision, so `commit` exists only where there is one and `why` only
 * where something changed. `Step.name` stays outside because every arm has it.
 */
export struct Step {
  string name;
  Action of;
}

/** The decision, with what it needs. */
export enum Action {
  /** The entry is valid: use this commit, and **do not** consult the ref. */
  Use(Commit commit),
  /** No entry for this mapping. An ordinary command may resolve the ref and write one. */
  Create,
  /** The entry records different inputs. Resolve again — and this is which input changed. */
  Refresh(Changed what),
}

/** Which manifest input stopped matching the lock. */
export enum Changed { Git, Ref, Subdir }

/**
 * One `Step` per mapping, in the manifest's order.
 *
 * Kept whole, including the sentence that is the rule: *"**A branch that moved is not a reason to do
 * anything.** An entry whose `git`, `ref` and `subdir` still match the manifest is `USE`, and the
 * commit it names is the answer — no lookup, no network, and the same answer next month. Only a
 * *manifest* change produces `REFRESH`; `wac update` is the separate, explicit operation and is not
 * this function."*
 */
export Vec<Step> plan(const Manifest m, const Lock l) { … }

/** Lock entries with no mapping — *"a mapping that is gone has nothing to decide."* */
export Vec<string> orphansOf(const Manifest m, const Lock l) { … }

export struct Manifest { Vec<Mapping> imports; }
export struct Mapping { string name; string git; string ref; string subdir; }
export struct Lock { Vec<LockEntry> entries; }
export struct LockEntry { string name; string git; string ref; string subdir; Commit commit; }

// ## What could not be written
//
// ### `Changed` is a guess, and the shipped field is better than it looks
//
// `why` is a `string` — *"for REFRESH, which input changed"* — and `Changed` above turns it into
// three variants. That is the move this directory makes reflexively and it is **not obviously right
// here**, because `why` is going to a human. A person reading *"the ref changed from `main` to
// `v2`"* is better served than one reading `Changed.Ref`, and reconstructing the first from the
// second means every caller formats it and they will not agree.
//
// The honest shape is probably both — a variant *carrying* the old and new values, so the message is
// derived rather than passed — and that is more than a rename. Left as three payload-free variants
// with this note, because writing `Refresh(Changed.Ref, string was, string now)` without a caller
// asking for it is the invention this directory is supposed to avoid.
//
// **The general form is worth having.** A `string why` is a fault's message, and
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has *a member with a `string` payload is a fault that has given up on being
// matched* from `http`. This is the first case where giving up looks correct: nothing branches on
// `why`, one thing prints it, and the set of reasons is open in practice because a future manifest
// field adds one. **The rule that entry needs is not *never a string* — it is that a `string` payload
// is right exactly when no caller will branch on it**, and that is a question about callers rather
// than about the type.
//
// ### Every mapping locks independently, and the type cannot say the entries are distinct
//
// `design/lang/0009` D10, quoted in the header: *"One entry per mapping, repeating the inputs and
// recording the full commit SHA, **even when several mappings name one repository** — the cache may
// deduplicate by repository and commit, but lock ownership stays per mapping, so updating one mapping
// cannot move another that happens to share a URL."*
//
// So `Lock.entries` is keyed by `name` and two entries with one name is a corrupt lockfile. `Vec` does
// not say that; neither would `Map<string, LockEntry>`, quite, because the *file* is a list and the
// duplicate has to be detected while reading it rather than prevented by the type. This is the sorted
// `Table` and the eight-element `Pieces` again — a property of a collection no collection type here
// carries — and the third form of it: not order, not length, but **uniqueness of a field**.
//
// ### And the two-part key next door, which `./resolve.wac` already raised
//
// `mapped(from, spec)` is a linear scan because *"references are comparable but not hashable"*, so a
// `Map` key must be built from two strings and `\0` does it. That entry's distinction is the useful
// part and belongs here too: **a composite key is cheap exactly when the lookup is allowed to be
// wrong.** `@/packages/wac`'s build cache hashes four things and a wrong lookup is a cache miss; in
// the resolver a wrong lookup is a wrong file.
//
// The lockfile is the third point on that line and the strictest: a wrong lookup here uses **another
// mapping's commit**, which is the exact failure D10 is written to prevent. So the same shape has
// three costs in one package — a miss, a wrong file, a wrong dependency — and nothing distinguishes
// them at the point where the key is built.
